1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,550
On the twenty-third of April 2025, India put the 1960 Indus Waters Treaty on hold.

2
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:14,514
Then, on the thirty-first of August 2026, a Court of Arbitration said India's reasons were not enough to suspend it.

3
00:00:14,514 --> 00:00:15,603
India's answer?

4
00:00:15,603 --> 00:00:17,577
Rejected, the very same day.

5
00:00:17,577 --> 00:00:20,980
So why does this treaty matter so much for India?

6
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:22,369
Let's find out.

7
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:24,969
Rewind to 1960.

8
00:00:24,969 --> 00:00:28,854
After nine long years of talks, brokered by the World Bank,

9
00:00:28,854 --> 00:00:36,470
Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru and Pakistan's President Ayub Khan signed the treaty in Karachi, that September.

10
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:41,707
And here's what makes it remarkable.

11
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:44,526
It survived the wars of 1965 and 1971.

12
00:00:44,526 --> 00:00:46,549
It survived Kargil in 1999.

13
00:00:46,549 --> 00:00:52,259
But in April 2025, after a terror attack in Pahalgam killed twenty-six people,

14
00:00:52,259 --> 00:00:59,497
India took a step it had never taken before: it announced the treaty would be held in abeyance.

15
00:01:02,333 --> 00:01:04,631
So what does the treaty actually do?

16
00:01:04,631 --> 00:01:05,997
It splits six rivers.

17
00:01:05,997 --> 00:01:11,957
The three eastern rivers, the Ravi, the Beas and the Sutlej, are India's to use without limits.

18
00:01:11,957 --> 00:01:16,800
The three western ones, the Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab, go to Pakistan.

19
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:22,440
India can still use them, but only in limited ways, like run-of-the-river hydropower.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,836
It also built a system to settle disputes.

21
00:01:27,836 --> 00:01:33,311
Step one: the Permanent Indus Commission, with one commissioner from each country.

22
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:35,158
Step two: a Neutral Expert.

23
00:01:35,158 --> 00:01:37,833
Step three: a Court of Arbitration.

24
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:44,976
So, what has India done since?

25
00:01:44,976 --> 00:01:49,363
From April 2025, it has flushed sediment from its dams on the Chenab,

26
00:01:49,363 --> 00:01:54,176
and put hydropower projects like Pakal Dul and Ratle on the fast track.

27
00:01:55,067 --> 00:01:56,437
Then came the twist.

28
00:01:56,437 --> 00:02:02,049
On the thirty-first of August 2026, the Court of Arbitration issued an interim award.

29
00:02:02,049 --> 00:02:06,486
Its message: India's grounds were not enough to suspend the treaty.

30
00:02:06,486 --> 00:02:10,180
And for now, it limited some construction at Ratle.

31
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:12,479
So, who says what?

32
00:02:12,479 --> 00:02:14,001
India's position is firm:

33
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:20,851
the treaty stays in abeyance until Pakistan credibly and irrevocably abjures its support for cross-border terrorism.

34
00:02:20,851 --> 00:02:28,118
India says this court was set up in breach of the treaty, has no jurisdiction, and India has never appeared before it.

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,853
Pakistan sees it the other way.

36
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:40,595
It says the award shows the treaty is fully in force and binding, and that India must comply.

37
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:45,952
Now, why should you care?

38
00:02:45,952 --> 00:02:49,254
Because three big things are at stake for India.

39
00:02:49,254 --> 00:02:50,265
One: security.

40
00:02:50,265 --> 00:02:54,241
India ties this treaty directly to cross-border terrorism.

41
00:02:54,241 --> 00:02:55,117
Two: energy.

42
00:02:55,117 --> 00:03:00,374
Fuller use of the western rivers means more hydropower for Jammu and Kashmir.

43
00:03:00,374 --> 00:03:01,857
And three: precedent.

44
00:03:01,857 --> 00:03:06,305
On the Brahmaputra, India is the downstream country, below China.

45
00:03:06,305 --> 00:03:10,176
So how river treaties are read matters to India too.

46
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:16,219
Okay, memory time!

47
00:03:16,219 --> 00:03:18,508
Here's your passport stamp.

48
00:03:18,508 --> 00:03:22,267
East is R-B-S: Ravi, Beas, Sutlej, for India.

49
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:26,517
West is I-J-C: Indus, Jhelum, Chenab, for Pakistan.

50
00:03:26,517 --> 00:03:27,661
And the year?

51
00:03:27,661 --> 00:03:31,803
Nineteen sixty, with the World Bank as broker.

52
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:36,710
Exam desk!

53
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:41,437
True or false: the treaty gives India unrestricted use of the Chenab.

54
00:03:41,437 --> 00:03:42,518
Think about it.

55
00:03:42,518 --> 00:03:42,991
False!

56
00:03:42,991 --> 00:03:45,084
The Chenab is a western river.

57
00:03:45,084 --> 00:03:49,572
India's unrestricted rights are on the Ravi, Beas and Sutlej.

58
00:03:51,067 --> 00:03:54,608
Next one: the treaty was brokered by the World Bank.

59
00:03:54,608 --> 00:03:55,008
True!

60
00:03:55,008 --> 00:03:57,914
Easy marks, if you remember the stamp.

61
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,339
And for Mains, practise this one: can water be an instrument of foreign policy?

62
00:04:04,339 --> 00:04:07,615
Discuss with reference to the Indus Waters Treaty.

63
00:04:07,615 --> 00:04:09,799
Build your answer in three parts.

64
00:04:09,799 --> 00:04:11,983
First, the treaty and its record.

65
00:04:11,983 --> 00:04:16,158
Second, India's security argument and Pakistan's legal argument.

66
00:04:16,158 --> 00:04:20,377
Third, the options ahead, from renegotiation to restoration.

67
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:24,601
As of November 2026, here's what to watch.

68
00:04:24,601 --> 00:04:28,928
Number one: does the Permanent Indus Commission meet again?

69
00:04:28,928 --> 00:04:34,408
Number two: India's hydropower projects on the Chenab, as they come online.

70
00:04:34,408 --> 00:04:40,550
And number three: the Neutral Expert's decision on Ratle, expected in July 2027.

71
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:49,459
And with that, a new thread joins our relations web: India and Pakistan, Indus Waters, in abeyance.

72
00:04:49,459 --> 00:04:52,805
Next briefing: India and Pakistan after Pahalgam.

73
00:04:52,805 --> 00:04:56,920
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

